00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:05.430 align:start position:0%
in power for 16 years angela merkel  wasn't afraid of confronting american  presidents  she stepped down as german chancellor in  december  replaced by olive schultz it meant a  fresh start in us german relations  something welcomed by a man who saw joe  biden's election as a good thing  for a while we feared that for the  previous government under president  trump it made no difference whether a  country was a democracy and a friendly  ally or not it is now clear what binds  us together  when it comes to international affairs i  think they very much come from the same  mindset they both  believe in the transatlantic alliance  also on russia basically the position is  very clear and very similar there should  be no change of borders in europe  it's not the first time the two have met  they spoke on the fringes of the g20  summit in rome in october when schultz  was finance minister and virtually after  he became chancellor they'll meet face  to face now with a looming international  crisis  we are all concerned about the situation  on the ukrainian border  with russian troops gathered on the  ukrainian border joe biden has promised  sanctions for any incursion but there's  a worry the russians might retaliate by  cutting off gas supplies to europe and  the hardest hit  will be germany the brunt of the  blowback costs from economic sanctions  whether it's financial sanctions on  banks whether it is energy sanctions  whether it is suspending north stream  two forever will be borne by the  europeans and by some europeans not by  all europeans that also has political  implications  olive schultz is the head of the  centre-left social democratic party as a  career politician who came late to the  top job he's been called the german joe  biden  the two have a lot in common shared  goals mutual objectives but schultz  knows it also plays well at home  when there's a german chancellor ready  to tell a u.s president where to go  alan fisher al jazeera washington
 

